
<p class="wp-block-paragraph">When I talk about productivity, I&#8217;m very opinionated about how important it is to protect your time.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">The whole productivity racket boils down to two pieces of advice:</p>



<ol class="wp-block-list">
<li>Figure out what&#8217;s important to you.</li>


<li>Be brutal with respect to everything else so you can work on what&#8217;s important to you.</li>
</ol>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">I can trace this advice back to Aristotle, but I&#8217;m sure people before him were saying the same thing.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">The thing is, however, these days I have a lot of control over my time. So I get a lot of email in the vein of &#8220;That&#8217;s easy for you &#8230;&#8221;,  but gang, that wasn&#8217;t always the case. </p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">From the years 1992 through 2021, I never had complete control of my schedule. I was a lawyer, answerable to clients, judges, and other lawyers. </p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Moreover, I was a trial lawyer, which meant I was never truly off the clock. I was always just one ex parte notice away from having my next week blown up, so I get it.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Today I&#8217;d like to share one trick I used to try and keep some sanity about my time in a world where I didn&#8217;t control my schedule. I called it the &#8220;desk list&#8221;. There was no fancy technology involved. It was a yellow pad, but on it, I had a list of all of my ongoing commitments.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">As a young lawyer, I worked for several senior lawyers, and each had projects they wanted me to take on that all seemed urgent to them. On the desk list, I kept a list of all my existing commitments and their due dates. It would say things like:</p>



<ul class="wp-block-list">
<li>Smith deposition on August 7</li>


<li>Jones summary judgment motion on August 22</li>


<li>Pennoyer v. Neff trial on September 13</li>
</ul>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">My list usually carried about 20 such items.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">I wrote it out by hand, and I always organized it in order of priority. The purpose of that list wasn&#8217;t for my reference, but for my defense. When someone walked into my office and wanted to throw one more piece of work on my desk, I pulled out the desk list and said, &#8220;Sure thing, I&#8217;ll do your work. I just need you to do me a favor and tell me where it fits on this list.&#8221;</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">If someone asked me to promote specific work, I needed them to tell me what other work would necessarily get demoted. If this new work they were going to give me was going to affect my time for a request from someone else, I would say, &#8220;That&#8217;s fine. Is it okay with John if I work on your motion instead of his trial?&#8221;</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Basically, I was enthusiastic about accepting work, but I held my assignor accountable for its impact. It was shocking to me how many times someone would look at my list, think silently for a moment, and say, &#8220;Actually, never mind. The stuff on your list is more important. I&#8217;ll get somebody else to do it.&#8221;</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">I secretly believe that this accountability over time had the additional impact that people didn&#8217;t bring me nonsense work anymore because they knew they&#8217;d have to account for it. </p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">I remember several occasions when someone would bring me a job and say, &#8220;Oh, I need you to do this. I already spoke to Matt, and he understands that this is more important than that trial you&#8217;re working on for him.&#8221;</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">In short, it reduced the amount of nonsense work assigned to me and added a small degree of sanity in the insane world of a trial lawyer.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">My request to you, as we head toward the end of the year, is this: if you feel like you don&#8217;t control your calendar and are often subject to the whims of others, put together your own desk list. Start holding the world accountable before it overloads you and completely prevents you from doing the things most important to you.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">In that process, hopefully you&#8217;re able to get toe-hold and time for those things most important to you.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">When it comes to protecting your time, this is a battle of inches, not yards. You&#8217;ve got to claw back every little bit at a time, but trust me, the struggle is worth it.</p>

